## What I did

A measured producer-parity fix, continuing the flag-cleanup arc with the highest-frequency remaining artifact.

**Measured the target first:** surveyed `unlace decompile` output and found the **parity flag (PF)** is the standout junk — `c11 = !(__builtin_popcount(LOBYTE(x)) & 1)`, **127 occurrences in medium.elf**. IDA essentially never emits PF unless a `jp`/`jnp` consumes it (rare). Confirmed it's the same register-reuse dead-store shape as the OF macros: in `main`, every `c11` reference is a write or `c11 = c11` self-assignment — **zero rvalue reads**.

**The fix:** generalized `segment_rhs_is_overflow_flag_macro` → `segment_rhs_is_pure_flag_store`, now also matching the balanced-paren `!(__builtin_popcount(...) & 1)` idiom (must consume the whole RHS; trailing consumers and popcount-as-value rejected). A later reassignment of such a flag register no longer keeps an earlier store alive; a genuine later rvalue read still pins it.

**Soundness, stated honestly:** PF is *not* a comparison flag, so it has no reconstruction theorem — its removal rests purely on standard dead-store elimination (overwrite-before-read). The OF macros additionally carry the `FlagReconstruct.v` `of_elim_sound` certificate. I updated the docs to draw that distinction precisely rather than overclaiming.

**Measured result:** `__builtin_popcount` **127 → 73 (−54)**, 37 fewer lines.

